1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,760
Good evening, excellence.

2
00:00:01,780 --> 00:00:03,230
Last episode’s riddle?

3
00:00:03,230 --> 00:00:06,040
The answer: the Balanced Scorecard.

4
00:00:06,040 --> 00:00:08,031
Four perspectives that show the whole

5
00:00:08,031 --> 00:00:09,690
company… not just the money.

6
00:00:09,690 --> 00:00:11,457
A company’s profits rose this

7
00:00:11,457 --> 00:00:12,870
year, and management celebrated.

8
00:00:12,870 --> 00:00:15,300
But that same year, complaints went up,

9
00:00:15,300 --> 00:00:17,111
its three best engineers left,

10
00:00:17,111 --> 00:00:18,560
and maintenance was postponed.

11
00:00:18,560 --> 00:00:20,155
The next year… profits

12
00:00:20,155 --> 00:00:21,750
collapsed all at once.

13
00:00:21,750 --> 00:00:23,160
Were the numbers lying?

14
00:00:23,160 --> 00:00:24,275
Or were we looking

15
00:00:24,275 --> 00:00:25,390
in the wrong place?

16
00:00:25,590 --> 00:00:27,773
A car has fuel, speed,

17
00:00:27,773 --> 00:00:29,520
temperature and oil gauges.

18
00:00:29,520 --> 00:00:31,210
If you only watch the fuel…

19
00:00:31,210 --> 00:00:33,660
the engine overheats without you noticing.

20
00:00:33,670 --> 00:00:35,670
The scorecard has four perspectives.

21
00:00:35,670 --> 00:00:37,600
Financial: are we profitable?

22
00:00:37,600 --> 00:00:39,920
Customer: satisfied, and coming back?

23
00:00:39,920 --> 00:00:42,650
Internal processes: is our work running right?

24
00:00:42,650 --> 00:00:44,322
Learning & growth: are our

25
00:00:44,322 --> 00:00:45,660
people learning and growing?

26
00:00:45,660 --> 00:00:47,030
The chain goes like this:

27
00:00:47,030 --> 00:00:49,310
skilled people do better work,

28
00:00:49,310 --> 00:00:50,415
which pleases the customer,

29
00:00:50,415 --> 00:00:51,520
and the money follows.

30
00:00:51,520 --> 00:00:52,690
So money is the

31
00:00:52,690 --> 00:00:53,860
last gauge to move…

32
00:00:53,860 --> 00:00:56,370
the other three warn you early.

33
00:00:56,370 --> 00:00:57,340
Try it tomorrow:

34
00:00:57,340 --> 00:00:58,712
take a sheet and split

35
00:00:58,712 --> 00:00:59,810
it into four squares.

36
00:00:59,810 --> 00:01:01,599
In each square, write one

37
00:01:01,599 --> 00:01:03,030
indicator you actually measure.

38
00:01:03,030 --> 00:01:04,947
An empty square… is the

39
00:01:04,947 --> 00:01:06,480
gauge you’re driving without.

40
00:01:06,480 --> 00:01:09,458
Kaplan and Norton introduced it in

41
00:01:09,458 --> 00:01:11,940
Harvard Business Review in 1992.

42
00:01:11,940 --> 00:01:14,140
Financial numbers tell you what happened…

43
00:01:14,140 --> 00:01:16,460
the other perspectives tell you what’s coming.

44
00:01:16,460 --> 00:01:17,910
Once you can measure… who

45
00:01:17,910 --> 00:01:19,360
carries the work with you?

46
00:01:19,360 --> 00:01:21,105
A manager carrying everything alone,

47
00:01:21,105 --> 00:01:22,850
while the team sits waiting.

48
00:01:22,850 --> 00:01:24,535
There’s a big difference between

49
00:01:24,535 --> 00:01:26,220
dumping work… and delegating it.

50
00:01:26,220 --> 00:01:27,090
Know the difference?

51
00:01:27,090 --> 00:01:28,310
Tell me in the comments.

52
00:01:28,310 --> 00:01:29,515
Save this episode, and

53
00:01:29,515 --> 00:01:30,720
see you next time

54
00:01:30,720 --> 00:01:32,690
on Masaa Al-Tamayoz.
